    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A South Indian dynasty that ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> for some six centuries, at its height the leading power of the south.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a> sacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#badami">Vatapi</a> in 642, during a long rivalry with the Chalukyas of Badami.</li><li>Its temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamallapuram">Mamallapuram</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> founded Dravidian architecture, and its script spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahendravarman-i">Mahendravarman I</a> wrote a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> comedy in which a drunken Shaiva ascetic accuses a Buddhist monk of stealing his skull begging bowl, which a dog had actually carried off.</p></aside>
    </section>

