    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#samudragupta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samudragupta.jpg" alt="Samudragupta" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#samudragupta">Samudragupta</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SamudraguptaCoin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PHGCOM, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pallavas first appear in the late third and fourth centuries CE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a>-plate charters written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#prakrit">Prakrit</a>, such as the Mayidavolu and Hirahadagalli grants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shivaskandavarman">Shivaskandavarman</a>, issued from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> and recording gifts of land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#andhra-region">Andhra</a> country and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ballari">Bellary</a> region. They emerged as the Satavahana dynasty and its successor in the east, the Ikshvaku dynasty, declined, and their heartland was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tondaimandalam">Tondaimandalam</a>, the country around Kanchipuram in the north of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>. Their origins are uncertain: an old theory linking them with the Iranian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pahlavas">Pahlavas</a> has little support, and most historians see them as a local or Andhra family that rose in Satavahana service. In the mid-fourth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#prayagraj">Allahabad</a> pillar inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#samudragupta">Samudragupta</a> lists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vishnugopa-of-kanchi">Vishnugopa of Kanchi</a> among the southern kings defeated during the Gupta ruler's southern campaign. Kanchipuram also features in the founding legend of the Kadamba dynasty: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mayurasharma">Mayurasharma</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brahmins">Brahmin</a> student there, is said to have taken up arms after being insulted by a Pallava horseman. For two centuries the dynasty, whose genealogy is poorly known, issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> charters and remained a regional power. It became a major state only when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#simhavishnu">Simhavishnu</a>, in the late sixth century, defeated the Kalabhras and extended Pallava rule south to the Kaveri delta, beginning the line usually called the later Pallavas.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#kanchipuram"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanchipuram.jpg" alt="Kanchipuram" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parameswara_Vinnagaram.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sridhar.selvaraj, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pallava kingdom was an agrarian society centred on the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tondaimandalam">Tondaimandalam</a> and the Kaveri delta, where irrigation tanks, many of them built or endowed by kings and local communities, sustained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a> cultivation. Royal charters granted villages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brahmins">Brahmins</a> as <em>brahmadeya</em> and to temples as <em>devadana</em>, spreading Brahmin settlements and temple estates that became centres of agricultural expansion and learning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> was a great city of temples, monasteries and colleges; the Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>, who visited around 640, described its many Buddhist monasteries, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hindu</a> temples and its numerous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jainism">Jains</a>. Its <em>ghatika</em>, a college of Brahmin learning, was renowned across the south. The Pallava age saw the decisive rise of devotional Hinduism in the Tamil country: the Shaiva <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nayanars">Nayanars</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#appar">Appar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sambandar">Sambandar</a>, and the Vaishnava <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alvars">Alvars</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thirumangai-alvar">Tirumangai Alvar</a>, composed Tamil hymns that drew worshippers to temples and turned many away from Jainism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buddhism">Buddhism</a>. Trade flowed through ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamallapuram">Mamallapuram</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, carried by merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guild">guilds</a>; a Tamil inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#takua-pa">Takua Pa</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thailand">Thailand</a>, which mentions a tank named after a title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nandivarman-iii">Nandivarman III</a> and a merchant guild, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manigramam">Manigramam</a></em>, attests to these links across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#pulakeshin-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii.jpg" alt="Pulakeshin II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pulakeshin_statue,_badami.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The defining conflict of Pallava history was a long struggle with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#badami">Badami</a> for supremacy in the south, fought alongside wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pandya-dynasty">Pandyas of Madurai</a>, the Gangas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#talakad">Talakad</a> and, later, the Rashtrakutas. Around 618–619 the Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahendravarman-i">Mahendravarman I</a> and advanced to the neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, although the Pallavas claimed a victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-pullalur">Pullalur</a>. Mahendravarman's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a>, known as Mamalla, turned the tide: he defeated Pulakeshin in several battles and in 642, with his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paranjothi">Paranjoti</a>, captured and sacked the Chalukya capital Vatapi, where Pulakeshin died, earning the title <em>Vatapikonda</em>, conqueror of Vatapi. He also sent two naval expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> to place his ally, the exiled prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manavanna">Manavamma</a>, on the throne. The Chalukyas recovered under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vikramaditya-i">Vikramaditya I</a>, and around 740 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vikramaditya-ii">Vikramaditya II</a> occupied Kanchipuram, though he spared its temples and left a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kannada">Kannada</a> inscription in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kailasanathar-temple-kanchipuram">Kailasanatha</a> temple recording his gifts. In the ninth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nandivarman-iii">Nandivarman III</a> defeated the Pandyas at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-tellaru">Battle of Tellaru</a>, but the Pandya wars continued until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-sripurambiyam">Battle of Sripurambiyam</a>, around 885, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aparajitavarman">Aparajita</a> and his Ganga ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#prithvipati-i">Prithvipati I</a> defeated the Pandya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#varaguna-ii">Varaguna II</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chola-empire">Chola</a> support, at the cost of Prithvipati's life.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#mattavilasa-prahasana"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mattavilasa-prahasana.jpg" alt="Mattavilasa Prahasana" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#mattavilasa-prahasana">Mattavilasa Prahasana</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mani_damodara_Chakyar-mattavilasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original uploaded by: Sreekanthv, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pallava kings ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hindu</a> monarchs in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> tradition, claiming descent from the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brahma">Brahma</a> through the epic hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashvatthama">Ashvatthama</a> and legitimating their power through Vedic sacrifices, temple building and grants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brahmins">Brahmins</a>. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a>-plate charters, first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#prakrit">Prakrit</a>, then in Sanskrit and, from the seventh century, in Sanskrit and Tamil, are the main sources for their administration. The kingdom was divided into provinces and districts, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tondaimandalam">Tondaimandalam</a> heartland into <em>kottams</em> and <em>nadus</em>, with royal officers overseeing revenue and justice while village assemblies, the <em>sabha</em> of Brahmin villages and the <em>ur</em> of other settlements, managed local affairs, tanks and temple endowments. Kings were patrons and practitioners of learning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahendravarman-i">Mahendravarman I</a> wrote the Sanskrit farce <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mattavilasa-prahasana">Mattavilasa Prahasana</a></em>, which satirises Buddhist monks and Shaiva ascetics, and bore titles such as Vichitrachitta, the curious-minded; tradition holds that the saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#appar">Appar</a> converted him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jainism">Jainism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shaivism">Shaivism</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#narasimhavarman-ii">Narasimhavarman II</a>, called Rajasimha, built the Shore Temple and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kailasanathar-temple-kanchipuram">Kailasanatha</a> temple and is associated with the poet and theorist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dandin">Dandin</a>. When the main line died out around 731, officials and leading men chose the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nandivarman-ii">Nandivarman II</a> from a collateral branch, an event thought to be shown in the sculptured panels of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vaikuntha-perumal-temple">Vaikuntha Perumal</a> temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#descent-of-the-ganges"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/descent-of-the-ganges.jpg" alt="Descent of the Ganges" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#descent-of-the-ganges">Descent of the Ganges</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Descent_of_the_Ganges_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pallavas transformed the architecture and art of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-india">South India</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahendravarman-i">Mahendravarman I</a> the first rock-cut shrines of the Tamil country were excavated, and his Mandagapattu inscription boasts of a temple made without brick, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timber">timber</a>, metal or mortar. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamallapuram">Mamallapuram</a>, the port named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a>, Pallava sculptors carved cave temples, the monolithic shrines known as the Five Rathas and the great relief usually called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#descent-of-the-ganges">Arjuna's Penance</a> or the Descent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ganges-plain">Ganges</a>; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#narasimhavarman-ii">Rajasimha</a> came the structural Shore Temple and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kailasanathar-temple-kanchipuram">Kailasanatha</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vaikuntha-perumal-temple">Vaikuntha Perumal</a> temple. These works, at Mamallapuram now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, laid the foundations of the Dravidian temple style later developed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chola-empire">Chola Empire</a>. Under Pallava patronage Kanchipuram, counted among the seven sacred cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hinduism</a>, flourished as a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> learning, and the bhakti hymns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nayanars">Nayanars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alvars">Alvars</a> composed in their age became the scriptures of Tamil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shaivism">Shaivism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a>. Beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, the Pallava form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#grantha-script">Grantha script</a>, used to write Sanskrit, was adopted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, where early inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cambodia">Cambodia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#champa">Champa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a> and the Malay world use scripts derived from it, and Pallava architectural forms influenced early temples in the region.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#aditya-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aditya-i.jpg" alt="Aditya I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#aditya-i">Aditya I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aditya_territories.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Venu62 (talk) (Uploads), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The later Pallava state was weakened by almost continuous war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> invasions in the eighth century, including the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> around 740, were followed by Rashtrakuta incursions, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pandya-dynasty">Pandyas of Madurai</a> pressed northward into the Kaveri delta. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nandivarman-ii">Nandivarman II</a> recovered and ruled for some sixty-five years, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nandivarman-iii">Nandivarman III</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-tellaru">Battle of Tellaru</a>, celebrated in the Tamil poem <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nandikkalambakam">Nandikkalambakam</a></em>, but disputes among his successors and the claims of rival branches divided the dynasty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-sripurambiyam">Battle of Sripurambiyam</a>, around 885, crushed Pandya power but left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aparajitavarman">Aparajita</a> dependent on his allies, among them the rising Chola dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>, which had grown up as an ally and subordinate of the Pallavas. Around 897 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chola-empire">Chola</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aditya-i">Aditya I</a> turned on Aparajita and defeated him; according to the later Chola <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tiruvalangadu-copper-plates">Tiruvalangadu plates</a>, Aditya killed Aparajita as he rode a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#war-elephant">war elephant</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tondaimandalam">Tondaimandalam</a> was annexed to the Chola Empire, which went on to dominate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-india">South India</a> for three centuries. Pallava descendants survived as minor chiefs, and in the thirteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kadava-dynasty">Kadava</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kopperunchinga-i">Kopperunjinga</a>, who claimed Pallava ancestry, briefly challenged the Cholas. The Pallava achievement lived on in the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamallapuram">Mamallapuram</a> and Kanchipuram and in the scripts and temple forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Pallava dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> ruled the northern Tamil country for six centuries, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#badami">Badami</a> for supremacy in the south, created the rock-cut and structural temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamallapuram">Mamallapuram</a> and Kanchipuram that founded Dravidian architecture, and spread a script that became the ancestor of many Southeast Asian alphabets.</em></p>

